#!/usr/bin/env perl
# aov() against R 4.6.1's aov(): the Type I table, the coefficients, the fitted
# values, and group_stats.
#
# PROVENANCE
#
# %DATA and %EXPECT are frozen output of t/aov.R.R under R 4.6.1 and MASS 7.3
# (re-run with `Rscript t/aov.R.R`).  The models are R's own:
#
#   npk_full    src/library/stats/man/aov.Rd: aov(yield ~ block + N*P*K, npk),
#               whose N:P:K is confounded with blocks.  The Rd sets Helmert
#               contrasts; the coefficients here are R's default
#               contr.treatment ones, the only coding the module has.
#   npk_npk     aov.Rd and tests/reg-tests-3.R:101 (PR#13505):
#               aov(yield ~ block + N * P + K, npk).
#   wb          src/library/datasets/man/warpbreaks.Rd: breaks ~ wool*tension.
#   br          tests/reg-tests-1b.R:2081: warpbreaks with the tension "M"
#               responses set to NA, breaks ~ wool + tension.  See %DIVERGE.
#   oats        tests/reg-tests-3.R:40 (PR#7829): MASS's oats[-1,],
#               Y ~ B + V + N + V:N, its row names "2".."72" passed in a
#               row_names column.
#   tg_slope    ToothGrowth (src/library/datasets/man/ToothGrowth.Rd), dose
#               numeric: len ~ supp + supp:dose, a slope per supplement.
#   tg_off      len ~ supp + offset(dose); R's fitted values include the offset.
#   dd, dd0     tests/reg-tests-2.R:3016 (PR#16437): num ~ F and num ~ 0 + F.
#   pg, pg_n    src/library/datasets/man/PlantGrowth.Rd's weight by group, in
#               the no-formula form: R's stack() of the named list, then
#               aov(values ~ ind).  pg_n names the groups "1", "10" and "2",
#               which up to 0.3213 were fitted as one numeric slope (Df 1).
#
# Through 0.3213 aov() refused npk_full (`N*P*K` crossed only its first `*`),
# tg_slope (an interaction without both main effects), tg_off (offset() read as
# a column) and dd0 lost a level of F.
#
# Factors are written as strings that do not look like numbers (npk's block
# "b1".."b6", N "N0"/"N1"), since a column of numbers is a covariate here.
# R's anova() leaves out a term with no estimable column; this module keeps it
# with Df 0, and %ALIASED lists where.  R's NA coefficient is NaN here.
#
# wb and br have wool and tension re-levelled alphabetically, the order the
# module gives a column of strings.  Coefficients are R's coef(fit, complete =
# TRUE): coef.aov() leaves an aliased one out, where lm() and this module keep
# it (as NA and NaN).
#
# DIVERGENCE (%DIVERGE).  A factor's levels are read from every row, not only
# from the rows the model keeps.  R's lm() builds its model frame with
# drop.unused.levels = TRUE, so in br, where every "M" row loses its response,
# R has no level M, and here M is an aliased column (coefficient NaN), is in
# xlevels, and is in group_stats with size 0 and a NaN mean.  The table is the
# same.  This is lm_design_build()'s, shared with lm(), glm() and anova(), and
# is pinned so that changing it is a deliberate act.
#
# group_stats is tapply(y, f, mean) and table(f) for each factor of the model,
# over the rows fitted: keyed by level when the model has one factor, by factor
# and then level when it has several.
#
# TOLERANCE
#
# 1e-9 relative on every sum of squares, mean square, F and p-value (absolute
# below 1e-300); 1e-9 relative to max(1, |R|) on coefficients, fitted values and
# group means, all of which are on the response's scale (1 to 120 here), so
# that a coefficient R gives as 1e-15 for an exact 0 is not compared
# relatively.  The fit is Givens rotations against R's Householder QR, so the
# two agree to rounding.  The worst disagreement is printed at the end: 2.4e-14
# on a double build (oats' B p-value), four orders inside the bound, which is
# the headroom for long-double and quadmath builds.

